        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "1def27bb-ac28-52e5-87fa-be22ce665135",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Malcolm Arnold, Queen Elizabeth II, Humphrey Searle, Oliver Messel\n- Organizations: Sadler's Wells Ballet\n- Places: Royal Opera House Covent Garden\n- Works: Homage to the Queen, Op. 42\n- Events: Coronation of Queen Elizabeth II\n\nFacts:\n- Oliver Hilary Sambourne Messel was born on 13 January 1904 and died on 13 July 1978.\n- Oliver Messel was an English artist and one of the foremost stage designers of the 20th century.\n- In 1953, Malcolm Arnold wrote Homage to the Queen, Op. 42 as the official coronation ballet.\n- In 1953, Sadler's Wells Ballet commissioned Homage to the Queen, Op. 42 in honour of Queen Elizabeth II.\n- Humphrey Searle, the musical adviser to Sadler's Wells Ballet, recommended Malcolm Arnold for the commission.\n- Homage to the Queen, Op. 42 was first performed by Sadler's Wells Ballet on Coronation night, 2 June 1953, at the Royal Opera House Covent Garden.\n- Oliver Messel created the original scenery and costumes for Homage to the Queen, Op. 42.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
